  <h1>PayFlow Assignment Write-Up</h1>

  <h2>Project Overview</h2>
  <p>PayFlow is a simplified UPI-style wallet backend API built with Spring Boot. It supports registering users, finding users, listing users by balance, and recording simple money transfer transactions. The project intentionally keeps the design simple for a beginner-to-mid level Spring Boot assignment.</p>

  <h2>How to Run the Application</h2>
  <pre>mvn spring-boot:run</pre>
  <p>H2 console URL: <code>http://localhost:8080/h2-console</code></p>
  <p>JDBC URL: <code>jdbc:h2:mem:payflowdb</code></p>

  <h2>Layer Explanation</h2>
  <p><b>Entity layer:</b> Contains Java classes that map to database tables. In this project, <code>User</code> maps to <code>users</code> and <code>Transaction</code> maps to <code>transactions</code>.</p>
  <p><b>Repository layer:</b> Contains interfaces that extend <code>JpaRepository</code>. Spring Data JPA creates the implementation automatically.</p>
  <p><b>Service layer:</b> Contains business methods used by controllers. The services call repositories directly in this simple assignment.</p>
  <p><b>Controller layer:</b> Contains REST API endpoints. Controllers receive HTTP requests, call services, and return Java objects that Spring converts to JSON.</p>

  <h2>Spring Boot Features</h2>
  <p><b>Embedded server:</b> PayFlow runs on embedded Tomcat, so no separate server installation is required.</p>
  <p><b>Auto-configuration:</b> Spring Boot configures Spring Web, JPA, H2, and repository beans based on project dependencies and properties.</p>
  <p><b>Production-ready defaults:</b> Spring Boot provides sensible defaults for startup, logging, configuration, and app behavior. Actuator can be added later if needed.</p>

  <h2>Generated Database Structure</h2>
  <p>H2 creates the database tables automatically from the JPA entity classes because <code>spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create</code> is configured.</p>

  <h3>Generated SQL for users</h3>
  <pre>create table users (
    balance float(53),
    user_id bigint generated by default as identity,
    name varchar(255),
    phone_number varchar(255),
    upi_id varchar(255) unique,
    primary key (user_id)
);</pre>

  <h3>Generated SQL for transactions</h3>
  <pre>create table transactions (
    amount float(53),
    transaction_id bigint generated by default as identity,
    note varchar(255),
    receiver_upi_id varchar(255),
    sender_upi_id varchar(255),
    primary key (transaction_id)
);</pre>

